<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palatinate (region)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhenish Palatinate, Lower Palatinate, Bavarian Palatinate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palatinate (Pfalz) is a historical region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It took its name from the counts palatine of the Rhine, Wittelsbach princes from 1214, whose lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> and the left bank of the Rhine were called the Rhenish or Lower Palatinate to distinguish them from the Upper Palatinate in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Its western part lies today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palatinate-region.jpg" alt="Palatinate (region)" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teilbereiche_der_Pfalz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Palatinate, in German Pfalz, occupies most of the southern quarter of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, with an area of 5,450 square kilometres. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a> in the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> range forms its north-western border, and the Upper Rhine marks the eastern border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. In the south the German-French border separates it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. One-third of the region is covered by the Palatinate Forest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s largest contiguous forested area. Its highest mountain is the Donnersberg, at 687 metres, and the German Wine Route passes through its wine region. Most of its major towns, among them Ludwigshafen, Speyer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenthal">Frankenthal</a> and Neustadt, lie in the lower eastern part, down to the Rhine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Celtic region, the area was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> under Augustus about 12 BCE and became part of Germania Superior. Alamannic tribes settled there in the decline of the Empire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> conquered them about 496. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1792 the Palatinate was divided into 45 secular and ecclesiastical territories, the largest being the Electorate of the Palatinate. By the late 12th century its count palatine had become a prince-elector, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> confirmed. The Bishopric of Speyer was the major ecclesiastical territory.</p><p>French revolutionary troops occupied the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1794, and in 1798 the Palatinate largely became the French department of Mont Tonnerre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 and a treaty of 14 April 1816 ceded most of it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, which made it a district with Speyer as capital, first called the Rheinkreis and, from 1835, the Pfalz. Because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, it kept a distinct legal status. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambach-festival">Hambach Festival</a> of 1832 near Neustadt became a milestone in German liberalism, and a separatist uprising in 1849 was suppressed by Prussian troops.</p><p>After the First World War French troops occupied the region, and French-backed separatist attempts between 1919 and 1923 ended in January 1924 with a treaty that guaranteed it would remain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Under Nazi rule it was merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a> into the Gau Westmark. In 1947 the French occupation authorities combined it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> and part of the Prussian Rhine Province to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Palatinate has about 1.4 million inhabitants, known as Palatines. The Diocese of Speyer and the Evangelical Church of the Palatinate still follow largely the boundaries of the old Bavarian district. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania-dutch">Pennsylvania Dutch</a> are descendants of Palatine immigrants, and the Pennsylvania Dutch language derives primarily from the Palatine German that Mennonite refugees brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> between 1717 and 1732. Its best-known dish is saumagen, a sow's stomach stuffed with pork, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and seasonings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palatinate (region)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Pavia of 1329 Emperor Louis IV the Bavarian divided the family lands, giving the <b>Rhenish Palatinate</b> and the Upper Palatinate north of Regensburg to his brother's sons Rudolf II and Rupert I, while Bavaria stayed with his own line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The final settlement came with the Treaty of Munich of 1816: Bavaria, which had already given back Tyrol and Vorarlberg and gained Würzburg and Aschaffenburg in 1814, returned Salzburg and the Innviertel to Austria and received the <b>Palatinate</b> on the left bank of the Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis II the Strict received Upper Bavaria, with Munich as residence, together with the <b>Rhenish Palatinate</b>, while his brother Henry XIII took the Duchy of Lower Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-upper-bavaria">Duchy of Upper Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
